-- title:
--   Integrality over A[j] forces q-expansion coefficients in A
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, a nonzero natural number $N$, a field $k$ of characteristic $q$ and a ring homomorphism $\mathrm{red} : A \to k$. Let $f$ be a Laurent series over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (a Hahn series over $\mathbb{Z}$) subject to two hypotheses. First, $f$ lies in `modularLocalized N A.toSubring red`: writing $R$ for the subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((\mathfrak{q}))$ generated by the constant series attached to elements of $A$ together with the two series `jqModC` and `jqNModC` (the former being $\mathfrak{q}^{-1}$ times the image in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((\mathfrak{q}))$ of the power series `jNum` $= E_4^3 \cdot$ `dedekindEtaUnitInv`), there are $r, s \in R$ with $s$ outside the prime ideal `redKer` of $R$ and $f \cdot s = r$. Second, $f$ is integral over the $A$-subalgebra generated by `jqModC`: there is a monic one-variable polynomial $P$ with coefficients in $A[X]$ such that substituting $\mathfrak{q}$-expansion values — each $a \in A$ going to its constant series, $X$ to `jqModC`, and the outer variable to $f$ — yields $0$. The conclusion is that $f$ already has all its coefficients in $A$: there exists $y \in A((\mathfrak{q}))$ whose coefficientwise image under the inclusion $A \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ equals $f$.
--
--   This is the descent of $\mathfrak{q}$-expansion coefficients to the valuation subring $A$ that underlies the reduction of modular curves in characteristic $q$, resting on the integral closedness of a Laurent series ring over a principal ideal domain; the cited input is [`ModularCurve.exists_coeffMap_eq_of_isIntegralElem_of_exists_eq_div_of_injective`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_coeffMap_eq_of_isIntegralElem_of_exists_eq_div_of_injective). It is used by [`ModularCurve.exists_fieldOver_lift_isIntegral_of_isIntegral`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_fieldOver_lift_isIntegral_of_isIntegral).
